syn-arcade big screen mode — tile glyphs
========================================

One SVG per value of the `icon` column that `syn-arcade big apps --rec` emits.
The file name IS the column value: big.c resolves <icon>.svg against this
directory and ships the resulting path as the `iconfile` column, so adding a
tile in apps_table() and dropping a drawing in here is the whole change — no
mapping table in the shell, and nothing to keep in step.

⚠ DRAWN, NOT BRANDED, and that is a deliberate decision rather than an artistic
one. The obvious set is each application's own logo — Steam's valve, Plex's
chevron, the Firefox fox. Those are trademarks: redrawing them puts a mark
somebody else owns into a GPL package, in a launcher that decides which of them
you see. Worse, they do not form a SET. Sixteen logos drawn by sixteen
different studios at sixteen different weights sit on one screen looking like a
list of other people's software, which is exactly what a console interface is
not. A glyph says what the tile DOES — a pad, a cartridge, a screen, a power
symbol — in one hand, and it is ours to change.

House rules, so a later addition matches:

  48x48 viewBox, no width/height attributes  scaled by the shell to whatever
                                             the tile is, which on a 4K
                                             television is not 48.
  stroke #cfc6ee, width 3, round cap+join    the ink, one step down from the
                                             label so the glyph reads UNDER
                                             the name rather than beside it.
  one accent detail in #a78bfa               the dendrite violet, and never
                                             more than one per drawing: the
                                             point of it is where the eye
                                             lands, and two of them is none.
  fill="none" on the outlines                a filled glyph goes black on a
                                             selected tile, which is lighter
                                             than an unselected one.

⚠ SVG TINY ONLY — no filters, no CSS, no <style>, no gradients. Qt renders
these through QtSvg, which is a Tiny 1.2 renderer: anything richer is not an
error, it simply does not draw, and the tile comes up blank on the television
while looking perfect in a browser.
